# NEGATIVE CONTROL for `zone_invariant at_rest`. NOT a test of the module.
#
# The `.fixture` suffix keeps it out of any *.rule glob: it is REQUIRED to
# fail, and a runner that picked it up as an ordinary rule file would report a
# permanent red.
#
# HOW IT IS RIGGED
#   The healthy zone brings varidx_inflight back to 0, which is what case 3 of
#   shm-coherence-varidx.rule asserts. This fixture demands == 1 instead.
#   Against a correct module that is unsatisfiable BY CONSTRUCTION -- so the
#   leg is deterministic, not probabilistic, and does not depend on catching
#   the counter mid-write.
#
#   Deterministic rather than probabilistic on purpose. The obvious
#   alternative -- assert `== 0` WITHOUT the quiesce and hope to catch a
#   transient non-zero reading -- would fire only sometimes, and a control that
#   fires only sometimes cannot distinguish "the oracle is broken" from "this
#   run was unlucky". A control has to be as reliable as the thing it certifies.
#
#   The quiesce is KEPT. Without it a red could be explained by the counter
#   simply not having settled yet, which is a timing artifact and not the
#   comparison firing. With it, the counter demonstrably settled and the
#   comparison then rejected the settled value -- which is precisely the
#   behaviour under certification.
#
# EXPECTED FAILURE MESSAGE (the driver greps for its stable prefix):
#   zone_invariant at_rest zone.varidx_inflight: have 0, want == 1
name    NEGATIVE CONTROL: at_rest must reject a settled counter that misses its bound
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Language: en\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   zone.varidx_inflight >= 0
quiesce zone.varidx_inflight 8000
fanout  24 2
zone_invariant at_rest zone.varidx_inflight == 1
